NAME
    ptr - console keyboard + pointer/mouse events (//#use ptr)

SYNOPSIS
    //#use abi          CONIN/CONOUT/CONST (required alongside ptr)
    //#use ptr          cc splices /lib/lib_ptr.c

DESCRIPTION
    One event source for interactive graphics programs: keys AND the
    mouse, both arriving on the raw console (CONIN). The mouse comes in
    as xterm SGR reports -- ptr_init() enables tracking (ESC[?1002h +
    ESC[?1006h) and asks the terminal its size (ESC[18t) so cell
    positions map onto the 480x272 panel. A terminal that answers
    neither still delivers every key, mapped as an 80x24 grid.

    Call ptr_init() once at start-up and ptr_done() on exit (it turns
    tracking back off, so the terminal stops emitting SGR after you
    quit). Then loop on ptr_ev(), which BLOCKS for the next event and
    returns its type.

    Coordinates are in PANEL space with the window sense the graphics
    engine uses: y is UP, 0..271, x 0..479.

EVENTS (ptr_ev return value)
    0   key         ptr_key = the character. Arrows arrive as 128..131
                    (up, down, right, left); other CSI reports are
                    swallowed.
    1   press       ptr_x / ptr_y = panel coords, left button down
    2   drag        button held and moved
    3   release
    4   right-click press
    5   free move    no button -- delivered ONLY after ptr_motion()
                    turns on 1003 tracking (off by default to spare
                    the console the motion flood)
    Wheel and other buttons are ignored.

FUNCTIONS
    ptr_init()          enable mouse tracking + query terminal size
    ptr_done()          disable tracking (CALL ON EXIT)
    ptr_ev()            block for the next event; returns the type above
    ptr_motion()        enable free-move (1003) events (type 5)
    ptr_motion_off()    disable free-move events
    ptr_key ptr_x ptr_y globals set by ptr_ev per the table above

NOTES
    The scripted-input trap is handled: the size query never eats a real
    keystroke (a non-ESC first byte is pushed back), so `p8xemu -i` runs
    can drive a client -- mouse included -- as printf'd bytes. On real
    PS/2 hardware the pointer is decoded by the ps2 library instead and
    integrated into the same panel coordinates; see man ps2.

SEE ALSO
